WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:04.040
Mind Your Decisions, Presh Talwalkar here.

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:14.400
Start with a circle. Draw a chord, and then draw another chord that makes a 30 degree angle with this chord, then draw another chord that's 30 degrees apart.

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:20.120
If the chord on the left has a length of 10, and the chord on the right has a length of 12,

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:25.019
what is the length of the chord in the middle whose value is x?

00:00:25.869 --> 00:00:28.679
I saw a similar problem on r/HomeworkHelp.

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:35.489
Pause the video if you'd like to give it a try ,and when you're ready, keep watching to learn how to solve this problem

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:52.809
We'll solve this problem by working in steps. First, focus on this part of the diagram.

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:58.690
This is a 30 degree inscribed angle corresponding to this arc of the circle.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:06.520
If we have another inscribed angle for the same arc, it'll have the same measure by the inscribed angle theorem.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:09.639
So this inscribed angle will be 30 degrees.

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:14.169
Now we'll do a similar thing for this inscribed angle.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:21.010
It corresponds to this arc of the circle; therefore,this inscribed angle will also measure 30 degrees.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:26.589
Now go back to the diagram and focus on this isosceles triangle.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:33.609
The sides opposite the 30 degree angles will be equal to each other, and let's say each of them has a value of y.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:39.280
We'll go back to the original diagram, and now we can focus on just this quadrilateral.

00:01:40.729 --> 00:01:43.029
We'll break it down into two different triangles.

00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:46.089
For the triangle on the right we can use

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:54.820
Al-Kashi's law of cosines to get that y^2 = 12^2 + x^2 - 2(12)x cos 30.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:04.060
We'll do a similar thing for this triangle,and we get y^2 = 10^2 + x^2 - 2(10)x cos 30.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.720
We now have a system of two equations.

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:13.989
We'll subtract the second equation from the first the y^2 terms will cancel out and x^2 terms will cancel out.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:23.540
So we're left with 0 = 12^2 - 10^2 and then we have to be careful here. We have -24x cos 30 degrees

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.100
minus a negative, so that'll become a positive

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:34.449
20x cos 30 degrees, so that results in -4x cos 30 degrees.

00:02:35.269 --> 00:02:42.729
We can now solve this equation. We rearrange the equation and then solve for x and that simplifies to be

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:50.350
22 divided by the square root of 3 and that's our answer. And if we go back to the original diagram,

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:56.320
there's another way that we can write it x = the simple average of 10 and 12

00:02:56.810 --> 00:03:01.930
Divided by cos of 30 degrees. In fact, we can generalize this formula!

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:07.539
Suppose we have a valid diagram with chords of a and b, and this angle is equal to Θ.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:16.440
You can actually prove using the same method in this video that x = 0.5(a+b)/cos Θ (the simple average of a and b)

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:24.820
The middle chord is the simple average, or arithmetic mean, multiplied by an adjustment factor of 1/cos Θ.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:31.210
It's like we've discovered a new type of average of how cord lengths progress in a circle!

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:36.009
It's not technically new, because someone probably will have discovered it thousands of years ago,

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:40.359
but it was new to me, and that's the joy of discovery in mathematics.

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:46.380
Thanks for making Mind Your Decisions one of the best channels on YouTube. As always,

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:49.540
thanks for watching, and thanks for your support.

